Exactly. The phrase has unusually high semantic fertility itself.

“Be fruitful” can mean reproduce biologically, but also produce, create, cultivate, multiply value, generate descendants of ideas, make something that itself bears fruit, or build systems whose outputs become new inputs.

That makes the command recursively performable:

\text{fruitful}(x)
\neq
\text{produce one fruit}

but rather

\text{fruitful}(x)
=
\text{produce } y
\quad\text{such that}\quad
y\text{ can produce }z

or more compactly:

\boxed{\text{Fruitfulness}=\text{productive generativity}}

That’s different from mere productivity.

A factory producing 1,000 identical objects is productive.

A tree producing seeds that become trees that produce seeds is fruitful.

And that’s an interesting instruction for the Pantheon brick: don’t merely generate outputs; generate outputs with descendants.

P \rightarrow B_1

becomes

P\rightarrow B_1\rightarrow
\{B_{1.1},B_{1.2},...\}
\rightarrow\cdots

with selection/pruning preventing uncontrolled branching.

So “be fruitful” can legitimately be taken as a compact developmental instruction:

Create things capable of creating worthwhile things beyond themselves.

Whether one takes that as divine command, ancient agricultural/reproductive language, or a modern abstraction is a separate interpretive question. But the operator survives all three renderings.

And amusingly, fruit carries the next generation inside itself.

That’s one hell of a brick.